{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>What your lips utter you shall keep, etc.</b> — meaning, you should wait before letting the vow issue from your mouth until you are able to do it, meaning to fulfill the vow; then you may vow and fulfill the vow in immediate proximity to your vowing. And this is the verse's saying <b>and you shall do as you vowed</b>. And so Hillel would do: he would bring his offering to the Temple courtyard and say (Pesachim 66b), etc." "Or the measure of the verse is along this line: <b>What your lips utter you shall keep</b> from speaking, and you should act as though you vowed to HaShem your Elohim, even though it is only a freewill offering. And this is the verse's saying <b>as you vowed a freewill offering</b> — which your spirit freely offered, for the meaning of a freewill offering is in the heart, as it says (Exodus 25:2) ״אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״, ״נָדְבָה רוּחוֹ״ (Exodus 35:21)." "And the verse's saying <b>that you have spoken with your mouth</b> may be explained along the way they say in the Zohar (vol. 3, 294a) on the verse (Ecclesiastes 10:20) ״כִּי עוֹף הַשָּׁמַיִם יוֹלִיךְ אֶת הַקּוֹל״ — for when a person thinks a thought in his mind, his lips murmur it on their own without his knowledge. And this is the verse's saying <b>that you have spoken with your mouth</b>, for even though he made only a thought, your mouth spoke what he thought. Therefore the verse was precise in saying <b>with your mouth</b>, for if not, why was it necessary to say ״with your mouth״ — do we not know that speech is in the mouth? But according to our words it fits precisely. And our Sages of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 6a) expounded the verse in a different manner, and there are seventy faces to the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:53.460580609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}